---
// Every claim below is sourced and spot-checked against the cited page directly (not just an
// agent's summary) before writing. Where research couldn't verify something, that gap is stated
// plainly rather than filled with a plausible guess.

// Model-year ranges verified directly against https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toyota_R_engine.
const TIMELINE = [
	{ label: "20R", start: 1975, end: 1980, note: "carbureted, this family's predecessor" },
	{ label: "22R", start: 1981, end: 1997, note: "carbureted" },
	{ label: "22R-E", start: 1983, end: 1997, note: "fuel-injected" },
	{ label: "22R-TE", start: 1985, end: 1988, note: "turbocharged" },
] as const;
const TIMELINE_MIN = 1975;
const TIMELINE_MAX = 1997;
const TIMELINE_SPAN = TIMELINE_MAX - TIMELINE_MIN;

const QUICK_FACTS = [
	{ label: "Block", value: "Cast iron" },
	{ label: "Head", value: "Aluminum, SOHC" },
	{ label: "Crank", value: "Forged steel" },
	{ label: "Timing", value: "Chain, not belt" },
	{ label: "Output", value: "~97-116 hp" },
	{ label: "Well-maintained life", value: "250k-300k mi" },
];

const MANUALS = [
	{
		label: "Operation CHARM",
		href: "https://charm.li/Toyota/1992/Pickup%202WD%20L4-144.4%202366cc%202.4L%20SOHC%20%2822R-E%29/",
		note: "Free factory service manual, matched to this exact configuration: 1992, 2WD, 2.4L SOHC, 22R-E.",
	},
	{
		label: "ether3al.com FSM mirror",
		href: "https://www.ether3al.com/93fsm-mobile/index.html",
		note: "Free, mobile-friendly, chapter-indexed factory service manual covering the broader 1989-1995 Pickup range.",
	},
	{
		label: "Toyota Truck & Land Cruiser Owner's Bible",
		href: "https://archive.org/details/toyotatrucklandc0000lude/page/n1/mode/2up",
		note: "Moses Ludel, 1995. A well-regarded narrative reference, more readable than a factory manual. Borrow through the Internet Archive.",
	},
	{
		label: "Chilton's Toyota Trucks, 1989-91 Repair Manual",
		href: "https://archive.org/details/chiltonstoyotatr00john",
		note: "Jim Taylor, 902 pages. Also an Internet Archive borrow.",
	},
	{
		label: "1992 Toyota Pickup engine manual",
		href: "https://www.scribd.com/document/411249668/ID67740f06b-1992-toyota-pickup-engine",
		note: "A scanned copy on Scribd. Reading past the preview needs a Scribd account.",
	},
];
---

<section id="engine-history" class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-16 md:px-12 md:py-20">
	<h2 class="font-anton text-4xl md:text-5xl">The engine, and why it's a big deal</h2>
	<p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		"22R-E" gets said like a badge of honor in these forums. Here's what's actually behind that reputation, and
		what isn't as well-documented as the folklore suggests.
	</p>

	<div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl">
		<div class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-soft">Quick facts</div>
		<div class="mt-3 grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
			{
				QUICK_FACTS.map((fact) => (
					<div class="raw-box rounded-xl bg-cream p-3">
						<div class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-soft">{fact.label}</div>
						<div class="font-display mt-1 text-base font-semibold text-ink">{fact.value}</div>
					</div>
				))
			}
		</div>
	</div>

	<div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl">
		<div class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-soft">Model-year timeline</div>
		<div class="mt-3 space-y-3">
			{
				TIMELINE.map((era) => {
					const leftPct = ((era.start - TIMELINE_MIN) / TIMELINE_SPAN) * 100;
					const widthPct = ((era.end - era.start) / TIMELINE_SPAN) * 100;
					return (
						<div>
							<div class="flex items-baseline justify-between text-xs text-ink-soft">
								<span class="font-display font-semibold text-ink">{era.label}</span>
								<span>{era.start}-{era.end} &middot; {era.note}</span>
							</div>
							<div class="mt-1 h-2.5 w-full bg-line/40">
								<div class="h-full bg-accent-blue" style={`margin-left: ${leftPct}%; width: ${Math.max(widthPct, 2)}%;`} />
							</div>
						</div>
					);
				})
			}
		</div>
	</div>

	<div class="mt-8 max-w-[65ch] space-y-8">
		<div>
			<h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">What makes it reliable</h3>
			<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
				A cast-iron block under an aluminum head is a design that tolerates overheating and repeated rebuilds far
				better than an all-aluminum engine. Add a forged steel crankshaft and a genuinely low, conservative state
				of tune, roughly 97-116 hp depending on year, and the bottom end never got stressed the way a high-output
				engine's would. It's a timing chain, not a belt, so there's no scheduled replacement interval forcing the
				issue. There's a real asterisk though: Toyota switched from a more durable double-row chain to a
				single-row chain with plastic guides starting with the 1983 model year, and those guides are the engine's
				actual known weak point.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
				The "runs to 500,000 miles" story gets repeated across nearly every forum and blog about this engine.
				It's real folklore: consistently repeated, technically plausible given the construction above, but not
				independently documented anywhere with a verified individual case. The better-supported number across
				sources is 250,000-300,000 miles for a well-maintained example. That's still excellent.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
				There is one verified exception: a 1980 Toyota Pickup, same single owner for 43 years, has documented
				over a million miles. Its original 20R engine carried the first 400,000 before being replaced with a
				2.4-liter 22R pulled from a 1983 model, which has since put on 600,000 more. It's not a purebred
				single-engine run, and one truck isn't a statistic, but it happened, and it's documented. Toyota has a
				genuine practice of taking an interest in trucks like this, just not this specific engine: when a 2007
				Tundra owner's original V8 passed a million miles in 2016, Toyota's chief truck engineer swapped him a
				brand-new truck for the old one, specifically to tear it down and study how it held up. It's the same
				buyback-for-research-and-bragging-rights instinct this engine's own reputation feeds off of, just
				demonstrated on a newer, different engine.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div>
			<h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">Where it showed up</h3>
			<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
				The carbureted 22R ran 1981-1997, the fuel-injected 22R-E from 1983 (fuel injection first appeared in
				August 1982) through 1997 alongside it, across the Toyota Pickup/Hilux, the Celica, the Corona, and the
				first two generations of 4Runner. A turbocharged 22R-TE existed too, 1985-1988, in the Hilux and 4Runner.
				Toyota also sold a rebadged version of this same-era truck as the Volkswagen Taro in Europe, 1989-1997,
				an unusual cross-brand arrangement. Per Toyota's own official history, 1981, the same year the 22R
				arrived, was also when 4WD was first offered on this compact truck line, with a 2-speed transfer case.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div>
			<h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">What it's called elsewhere</h3>
			<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
				In Japan, this truck has been sold continuously as the Hilux since 1969. North America dropped that name
				entirely for this generation, marketing it simply as the Toyota Truck (commonly called the Toyota Pickup,
				both names show up). When the SUV wagon variant launched in 1984, it kept the Hilux Surf name in Japan but
				became the 4Runner everywhere else, the same vehicle, a different name depending on where you bought it.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div>
			<h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">Keeping it fed</h3>
			<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
				<a href="https://www.lceperformance.com/" class="text-accent-blue underline underline-offset-2">LCE Performance</a>
				 (LC Engineering) has specialized in Toyota-truck parts since 1985. <a href="https://22reperformance.com/" class="text-accent-blue underline underline-offset-2">22RE Performance</a>,
				a Chico, California shop, focuses specifically on 22R/22R-E internals. For timing components specifically,
				forum consensus points toward Cloyes and Tsubakimoto as the established quality timing-chain makers and OSK
				as a well-regarded budget timing-kit brand, with experienced builders often recommending an OEM chain paired
				with aftermarket metal (not plastic) guides bought separately, rather than a generic boxed kit. Aisin and
				Denso come up repeatedly as the preferred OEM-equivalent brands for water pumps, starters, and similar
				electrical wear parts.
			</p>

			<div class="mt-6">
				<div class="font-mono text-[11px] uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-soft">Manuals worth checking first</div>
				<div class="mt-2 divide-y-2 divide-black raw-box rounded-xl bg-cream">
					{
						MANUALS.map((manual) => (
							<div class="p-3">
								<a href={manual.href} class="font-display text-sm font-semibold text-accent-blue underline underline-offset-2">
									{manual.label}
								</a>
								<p class="mt-1 text-xs leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">{manual.note}</p>
							</div>
						))
					}
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div>
			<h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">Notable builds</h3>
			<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
				There isn't one single, universally-cited "famous" 22R/22R-E build the way some platforms have a legendary
				reference car, this is more an active, ongoing community of many builds than one landmark project.
				<a href="https://www.gregstoyotarestorations.com/highlights-to-fame/" class="text-accent-blue underline underline-offset-2">Greg's Toyota Restorations</a>,
				a shop specializing in classic Toyota pickups and Land Cruisers, has placed in SEMA's Battle of the
				Builders.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
				A recurring, genuinely fun genre within that community: replica builds of Marty McFly's 1985 SR5 from
				Back to the Future. Builder Alan, who posts as
				<a href="https://www.instagram.com/alwaystoyota/" class="text-accent-blue underline underline-offset-2">@alwaystoyota</a>,
				has built eight of these tribute trucks out of real 1985-1987 pickups. One, a full frame-off restoration
				built for the off-road lighting company KC HiLiTES, appeared in a 2023 Toyota Tundra commercial and their
				booth at an off-road expo, a small but real sign of how far this specific build genre has traveled. A
				separate McFly-truck build sold for $58,000 on Bring a Trailer in 2020, at the time reported as the most
				expensive Toyota pickup ever sold there.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div>
			<h3 class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">Where did they all go?</h3>
			<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
				If the engine is this durable, why aren't more of these trucks still on the road? The answer isn't the
				engine. It's the frame. Toyota's box-frame design on these trucks traps road debris and moisture inside
				the rails themselves. The rear crossmember, cab mounts, and shock crossmember rust from the inside out,
				often badly, well before a properly maintained 22R/22R-E is anywhere near done. It's common enough that a
				whole aftermarket product category exists just for it: companies like Rust Buster sell dedicated
				rear-frame repair kits specifically for these years. That wouldn't be a real business if the problem
				weren't real and common.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
				Beyond the frame, this part is more my own reasoning than a cited statistic: these were inexpensive work
				trucks for most of their lives, not collectibles. A truck with frame rust, an accident, or a botched DIY
				lift kit usually got scrapped rather than properly repaired, because the truck itself was never worth
				enough to justify the bill. A weekend lift kit or budget aftermarket parts, installed without the
				fabrication a rusty frame or worn suspension mount actually needs, can shorten a truck's working life
				considerably even while the engine underneath keeps running fine. The engine's reputation for outliving
				everything around it is, in a real sense, survivorship bias. The trucks people kept babying are the ones
				still around to be admired, and they're a small, self-selected slice of everything Toyota built.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
